"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nCt. App. 2015) (refusing to enforce the terms at issue by noting that, unlike the consumers in Hill,\nconsumer in this case had neither notice of the terms nor opportunity to reject by returning the\ngoods); M.A. Mortenson Co. v. Timberline Software Corp., 998 P.2d 305, 308 (Wash. 2000)\n(concluding that Washington allows the formation of \"layered contracts\" similar to those\nenvisioned by ProCD, Hill, and Brower); Noble v. Samsung Elecs. Am., Inc., 682 F. App'x 113\n(3d Cir. 2017) (finding that, unlike in Hill and ProCD, consumers in this case were not given\nreasonable notice that additional terms were included in the box); Hill v. Gateway 2000, Inc., 105\nF.3d 1147 (7th Cir. 1997) (overturning a refusal to compel arbitration because consumers were\nbound by the arbitration clause contained in the materials shipped to and accepted by consumers);\nFeeney v. Dell Inc., 87 Mass. App. Ct. 1137, 2015 WL 4460182 (Mass. App. Ct. July 27, 2015)\n(concluding that the terms and conditions included in the box shipped with computer post purchase\nwere part of the original agreement because consumer was given a reasonable notice and a\nmeaningful opportunity to reject); Norcia v. Samsung Telecomms. Am., LLC, 845 F.3d 1279 (9th\nCir. 2017) (refusing to enforce the terms in the box because notice was insufficient); Velasquez-\nReyes v. Samsung Elecs. Am., Inc. 777 F. App'x 241 (9th Cir. 2019) (finding that consumer did\nnot have reasonable notice of terms printed on the box, because store employee opened the package\nwith the product, so consumer could not see the terms); Starke v. SquareTrade, Inc., 913 F.3d 279\n(2d. Cir. 2019) (finding that notice of the terms was not conspicuous enough to provide a\nreasonable notice of the later arriving terms); Dye v. Tamko Bldg. Prods., Inc., 2021 WL 5578736\n(D.N.J. Nov. 29, 2021) (finding that consumer was bound to the warranty terms written on\nproduct's packaging because it was conspicuously disclosed).\nFor cases in which courts refused to enforce late-arriving terms, see Deering, Milliken & \nCo. v. Drexler, 216 F.2d 116 (5th Cir. 1954) (ruling that invoice accompanying shipment did not\noperate to modify original contract by including arbitration provision); A.B.C. Home & Real\nEstate Inspection, Inc. v. Plummer, 500 N.E.2d 1257 (Ind. Ct. App. 1986) (determining that\ninspector's exculpatory clause in inspection report delivered to buyers after the inspection was\nineffective to modify inspector's warranty); Bowdoin v. Showell Growers, 817 F.2d 1543 (11th\nCir. 1987); Sanco, Inc. v. Ford Motor Co., 771 F.2d 1081, 1086 (7th Cir. 1985); Klocek v.\nGateway, Inc., 104 F. Supp. 2d 1332, 1341 (D. Kan. 2000); Whitaker v. Farmhand, Inc., 567 P.2d\n916 (Mont. 1977) (finding that disclaimer, which was received by farm owners subsequent to sale,\ndid not limit plaintiff's recovery for the warranties); Rogers v. Dell Comput. Corp., 138 P.3d 826\n(Okla. 2005) (deciding that, in a case in which seller sought to enforce an arbitration provision\nallegedly sent with an invoice with the purchased computer, the record was insufficient to allow\nthe state supreme court to determine whether the arbitration provision was part of the contract and\nenforceable).\nConsidering time trends further bolsters the conclusion that enforcement of PNTLs is the\ndominant approach. Prior to Hill, only one case, Sanco, enforced the PNTL contract. Subsequent\nto Hill, however, all but one case, Klocek, embraced the approach set forth in this Section (with\nvarying degrees over time) with respect to \"terms later\" contracts. This becomes even clearer when\nconsidering all available published and unpublished PNTL cases involving consumer contracts in\n69\n@§2\n"